# Visual design — Soft Adventure

The visual identity is **Soft Adventure**, evolving toward a cohesive **Little Adventure World**. Pair with [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc) (environment, props, layers, composition), [child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc) (play UX), [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc) (touch, feedback, implementation), and [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc) (canonical character).

## Child-facing UI should feel

- Playful
- Warm
- Friendly
- Calm
- Colourful
- Tactile

## Avoid

- Corporate SaaS aesthetics
- Dense dashboards
- Excessive gradients
- Excessive shadows
- Neon colours
- Excessive animation
- Sharp rectangular UI (hard 0-radius boxes, rigid grid chrome)
- Tiny controls
- Visually noisy screens

## Use

- Rounded shapes (buttons, cards, panels, inputs)
- Generous whitespace
- Large typography
- Simple illustrations
- Soft colours
- Clear visual hierarchy (one focal element per screen when possible)

## Typography

- Child-facing content: use a **friendly rounded typeface** such as **Fredoka** (or the project’s designated child font token).
- Keep on-screen type minimal; size for tablet reading distance, not desktop density.

## Colour and tokens

- Use the project’s **centralized design tokens** for colours (CSS variables, theme config, or shared token module — wherever the repo defines them).
- Do **not** hardcode one-off hex/rgb/hsl values inside child-facing components; extend tokens when a new colour is needed.
- Prefer soft, warm hues; reserve strong saturation for small accents and success moments, not full-screen backgrounds.

## Component strategy (child vs parent)

- **Child-facing UI must not look like a default shadcn/ui application** (neutral greys, tight radius, small text, dense layouts).
- **shadcn/ui** is acceptable for **parent/guardian/admin** surfaces and for **functional primitives** behind the scenes.
- **Child-facing components** should be **custom-designed** for Soft Adventure: rounded, tactile, illustration-friendly, token-driven.

## Little Adventure World (direction)

Over time, child-facing visuals should feel like one **inviting illustrated world**, not a loose stack of generic cards or **polished character on geometric CSS/SVG scenery**. Full design rules live in [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc): character + illustrated environment + props + interaction layer + existing learning UI, with a consistent depth model and tablet-first hierarchy.

**Visual coherence** between character and environment (illustration language, softness, lighting, depth, colour harmony) is a product priority — **above** choosing CSS/SVG for implementation convenience.

**Learning clarity comes first.** World-building supports the task; it must not compete with choices, targets, drag areas, or celebration focus.

Environment palette harmonizes with Soft Adventure; world rule documents reference hex anchors — prefer **tokens** in code ([Colour and tokens](#colour-and-tokens) above) where UI applies; illustrated environments may use closely related natural colours in artwork. Do **not** build prop libraries, scene engines, or visual asset registries until explicitly requested.

Cohesive scenes may use **dedicated environment illustration assets** (environment only — no character, no UI baked in). See [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc).

## Walk Through the Week

Walk Through the Week is **world-first, not card-first**: the environment should **own the viewport** (one continuous scene), with Home, Today/weekdays, Audio, and the banner **layered into** the sky — not stacked above a centered image with page margins. Utility chrome stays small and secondary.

The **Day/Event banner** is a **first-class illustrated UI asset** (reusable raster frame at `public/environments/walk-through-week/day-event-banner.png`) composited with **dynamic HTML** day/event text — not a white card, shadcn button, or CSS gradient plaque. It can be the **child tap target** to move the week forward, using a calm gold glow when ready (glow in the app layer, not baked into the PNG). Same banner frame for every weekday/event; environments change per event.

Approved composition reference: `docs/design-references/walk-through-week/school-day-reference.png` (not a runtime asset). Reject white lesson cards and “UI column + gap + small environment” layouts — see [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc) §16. **Reuse visual language across days; do not force identical physical composition** on Chai Shop, Cake, or Park.

## Character artwork

Production character moods are **illustrated raster assets** behind **`LittleAdventureCharacter`** (shell + figure). Keep **one** canonical character component; swap or refine **figure** artwork without forking per-game mascots. Environment artwork is **separate** and composited beneath the character.

## Motion and depth (visual)

- Depth: light, soft elevation — not stacked drop shadows or glassmorphism stacks.
- Motion: supports the calm, playful feel; see child-experience for intensity limits (gentle, not flashy).
